{
  "title": "Inventory what the server does at startup and explain why, before cleaning it up",
  "description": "server/src/main.rs (about lines 190-260) and db.rs now run a growing list of work before the server accepts requests: Diesel migrations, Rust data migrations (data_migrations.rs: rewrites every recipe for every user in one transaction, using live parser and catalog code), photo backfill, resume_interrupted_jobs (now capped at 8 concurrent jobs), ingredient-name sync_with_catalog (candidates() over 10k+ keys per stored row), queue_stored (re-estimates every stored recipe), touching every live shopping item when categorizer_version changes, and reask_other_models. \
Most `.expect()` and abort startup on failure, so startup time and fragility grow with the data. \
Step 1 (this issue): write up an inventory for the owner: every startup task, what it does, why it exists (with the commit or issue that added it), how its cost scales, what happens if it fails, and whether it must block serving. \
Recommend for each: delete, run once and remove (one-off data migrations already applied in prod), move to a background task after the server is listening, or keep. \
Make no code changes until the owner has read the inventory and agreed which to change; then file follow-up issues for the approved changes.",
